Second Part
Form and Mechanism of Services for Violence Against Women and Children Article 21 (1) In the context of providing protection services to women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government establishes the Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children (UPTD PPA), Integrated Referral Service System (SLRT), Task Force on Violence Against Women/Children (Tim KTP/A) in community health centers, Hospitals, schools, Islamic Boarding Schools, Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA).
(2) The Regional Government through UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to implement services:
a. Public complaints;
b. Victim outreach;
c. Case management;
d. Temporary shelter;
e. Mediation;
f. Victim assistance; and
g. Victim repatriation.
(3) Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation. (4) To assist in the protection of women and children, the Regional Government establishes:
a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Task Force for Child-Friendly Regency;
d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor or Worst Forms of Child Labor (BPTA); e. Regional Children's Forum (FAD);
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM) PENA BISMA;
g. Domestic Violence Forum (KDRT);
h. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children (Satgas PPA); i. Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA);
j. TIM KTP/A in Hospitals, Community health centers, schools, Islamic Boarding Schools.
(5) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the task forces as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER IV
FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN Article 4 Forms of violence against women and children, among others: a. Physical violence;
b. Psychological violence;
c. Sexual violence;
d. Neglect;
e. Exploitation;
f. Other violence, namely:
1) Victims of pornography;
2) Victims of disaster and conflict;
3) Victims of narcotics;
4) Victims infected with HIV/AIDS;
5) Victims of terrorism;
6) Victims of Stigmatization;
7) Worst Forms of Child Labor (WFCL);
8) Street children and deviant behavior;
9) Persons with disabilities and/or;
10) Children in Need of Special Protection and others.

Article 31
(1) Funding for the protection of women and children victims of violence includes:
a. Medical services, which include doctor's examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs;
b. Medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and et Psikiatrikum;
c. Psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. Legal services;
e. Economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
(2) Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is conducted transparently and can be legally accounted for.

Article 9
Exploitation as referred to in Article 4 letter e includes: a. Acts of economic or sexual exploitation with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
b. Acts with or without the consent of the victim which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or capabilities of a person by other parties to obtain benefits both material and immaterial.

REGENT OF TANGGAMUS,

Considering : a. that in order to protect the constitutional rights of women and children in accordance with the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the Regional Government is obliged to be responsible in organizing the protection of women and children against acts of violence;
b. that to fulfill the indicators of the Implementation of Child-Friendly Regency, concerning protection services, prevention of violence against women and children, regulations are needed to regulate the various roles of regional apparatus institutions and other institutions;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Organization of Protection of Women and Children;

In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 16 of 2019 Amendment as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 16 of 2019 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 186, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6401);
